-var VERSION = "0.28.16-codeagent"; // 0.28.14 SIGNAL-SQL-ARITY-1 (#2779, 2026-10-19, RESOLVE-ALL-OPEN-1): stallDetector bound one value to "UPDATE issue_triage SET close_evidence=?1 WHERE issue_id=?2", the one prepare/bind site in this file whose placeholder count differs from its binds (static scan of 150 sites); it now binds the issue id and upserts the triage row, so a recovered stall closes its Q08-PUBLISH-STALL-AUTO-1 issue. // Q08-YIELD-ORDER-1 (2026-10-18, pillar research). Measured 7-day publish yield from engine_runs: nemotron 12.8%, gpt-oss 3.8%, kimi 1.7%. COMPOSE_MODELS led with kimi (lowest yield, highest cost, ~1040 neurons/call); reordered to nemotron, gpt-oss, kimi. Raises q08_gate_pass_rate_7d and lowers q08_neurons_per_published_piece_7d together. 0.28.1 Q08-PRUNE-EXTEND-1 (agent_issues 2284; 29 of 77 failed runs in the 48 h to 2026-10-14 13:30 UTC ended "too short after pruning"): when deleting the flagged sentences leaves a clean draft whose only problem is the 2800-character floor, the one model revision extends that pruned draft from the source (every surviving sentence kept, the deleted claims named as not to be restated) instead of rewriting the unpruned draft, and the result is re-verified in full. 0.28.0 EXTERNAL-PROPERTIES-1 (owner 2026-10-14: '"By property" SHALL ALSO INCLUDE Q08.ORG AND READING.Q08.ORG'): every tick ends by mirroring the finished runs of the last 7 days (published or rejected at the gate, title and stage only) into qnfo-audit.content_external_items, so fleet.qnfo.org/content shows q08.org by property. 0.27.0 (owner 2026-10-14: "REJECTION RATES ARE UNACCEPTABLY HIGH ... WHY ZERO PUBLISHED TODAY?"; engine_runs 394-417: 2 of 24 published, 13 of 16 failed essays stopped on a flagged lede claim): Q08-LEDE-PRUNE-2 (a flagged lede sentence is cut when another lede sentence remains; ledeProblems still guards the opening) and Q08-GROUND-PROVENANCE-1 (the fact-check ground states the item's title and the site it appeared on as sentences, and the reviewers are told the source line is source material; "the paper appears on arxiv.org" was flagged unsupported in runs 415 and 417). 0.26.1 Q08-TITLE-REPAIR-2: every attempted title repair leaves a q08_reader_tests trail note (the reply opening or the error; runs 405 and 406 left none), and a title wrapped in a preamble, a Title: label or a code fence is read. 0.26.0 Q08-TITLE-REPAIR-1 (agent_issues 2284, 2503): a writer draft whose only writer-gate problems are its title gets one short title call to the same writer before the next writer is tried (8 of 15 nemotron drafts in a day were refused for the title alone while nemotron wrote every published piece). // 0.25.1 WORKERS-BP-LOGGING-1 (#2342): swallowed catches log; Q08-PANEL-METRIC-STATE-1 (#1895): the panel effective-votes and panel-unavailable writes set state MEASURED. // 0.25.0 Q08-ENTITY-BACKGROUND-1: the products, projects and organisations a headline names get their own full Wikipedia article in the background, so the system the analysis explains is in the checked source (run 390). 0.24.2 was: // 0.24.2 Q08-PRUNE-BODY-FLOOR-1 + Q08-SOURCE-CONCESSION-1: the prune floor is the publish floor measured on the body, and a fact-check item whose reason concedes it is in the source material (FAQ, comments, background) is dropped (run 388). 0.24.1 was: // 0.24.1 Q08-PARAPHRASE-ITEMS-1: in the reporting, a fact-check item whose reason concedes the content (phrasing, not explicitly stated, implied) and whose sentence has no digit, quotation or name does not fail the essay (run 387). 0.24.0 was: // 0.24.0 Q08-BUDGET-ROUTE-1: while the workers-ai or total ai_spend cap is breached the writer order puts nemotron and gpt-oss before kimi-k2.6 (BUDGET-SOFT-ROUTE-1, issue 1780). 0.23.2 was: // 0.23.2 CRON-ONLY-HEARTBEAT-1 (agent_issues 2397, pillar autonomy): each hourly scheduled run ends with a qnfo-audit fleet_heartbeat upsert (ok=0 when generation threw); write errors are logged. // 0.23.1 Q08-NAME-COMPOUND-1 + Q08-COMPOSE-TRAIL-1 (each writer's gate result is kept in q08_reader_tests): in a hyphenated compound only the capitalised parts are checked as names ("AI-driven", "Claude-generated"; run 386). 0.23.0 0.23.0 Q08-TECH-SYSTEMS-1: the analysis explains the technical system (components, data flow, the design decision or constraint behind the event, the trade-off) instead of an incentive template; the background reads full Wikipedia articles on the technologies; both reader prompts judge technical understanding. 0.22.1 0.22.1 Q08-PRUNE-FLOOR-2: the prune floor is the publish floor (3000 characters with title and verdict), so the repair stops instead of eroding a draft that can no longer publish (run 385). 0.22.0 0.22.0 Q08-WRITER-UPGRADE-1: kimi-k2.6 writes first with thinking off, nemotron and gpt-oss as per-request fallback. 0.21.0 0.21.0 Q08-FINAL-READ-1 + Q08-SOURCE-DEPTH-1 (eight comments and arXiv abstracts up to 3000 characters; the fact-checkers count background and comments as source): the exact text that would be published needs 2800 characters of body and a final two-family reader panel (vacuity-focused prompt, mean 4, no judge under 3, fails closed) after the fact check and its prunes. 0.20.3 0.20.3 Q08-ANALYSIS-CONCRETE-1: an analysis-check item counts only when the sentence it quotes has a digit, a quotation or a name; abstract reasoning flagged against the reviewers' own instruction is dropped (run 383). 0.20.2 0.20.2 Q08-VERDICT-NORM-1: near-miss verdict lines are normalised, and a first draft whose only problem is the verdict line gets the verdict micro-call (which now reads the essay) before a full rewrite (run 382). 0.20.1 0.20.1 Q08-MARK-SHIFT-1: an analysis marker placed where the guard rails refuse it moves later, paragraph by paragraph, to the first accepted place instead of sending the whole essay to the strict check (run 381). 0.20.0 0.20.0 Q08-ANALYSIS-DEPTH-1: an essay publishes only with a systems analysis of at least two paragraphs and 700 characters whose opening refers to something the piece said (a thin one goes to the one model revision); the inference prune keeps the analysis section (Q08-ANALYSIS-KEEP-1), and a "This <noun>" sentence left after a cut goes with it (Q08-DANGLING-CUT-1). 0.19.3 0.19.3 Q08-SOURCE-ORDER-1: Hacker News stories and arXiv papers are tried before GitHub repositories (alternating, never the last source twice); a repository is used only when no story or paper ahead of it has enough source material (runs 354, 356, 358, 375, 379: repository essays had no system to analyse). // 0.19.2 Q08-ANALYSIS-AUTO-1 (run 378): when the writer leaves the <!-- analysis --> marker out, the analysis starts at the first body paragraph after the lede and context written in reasoning language (same 60% and 600-character guard rails, still checked for invented facts); the run error records the analysis split (marked, auto, none). // 0.19.1 Q08-SOURCE-GROUND-1 (run 377): the fact-check ground carries the source description the writer is given (kind, title, link, site) and labels each part (discussion comments, linked article, background), so "posted on apple.com" and "a commenter on Hacker News said" can be verified instead of failing as not in source. // 0.19.0 Q08-ANALYSIS-SECTION-1 (owner 2026-10-13: big-picture systems analysis): the writer marks where its systems analysis starts with an invisible <!-- analysis --> line; the reporting before it keeps the sentence-by-sentence fact-check, the analysis after it is checked only for concrete invented facts (events, named parties acting, dates, numbers, quotations, studies), the deterministic name/year/figure check still covers the whole text, the analysis may be at most 60% of the body, and the marker never reaches readers or the published page. // 0.18.2 (run 375): "README", "CHANGELOG", "GitHub" and "repository" are not names the grounding check needs in the source (the source of a repository piece is its README); the fact revision is told to keep a news lede naming the subject and where it appeared. // 0.18.1 Q08-LEDE-PRUNE-1: the fact-check prune never deletes sentences of the first paragraph (a piece was published opening "Users therefore trust ..." after its lede was pruned); a flagged claim in the lede sends the draft to the model revision, and every pruned draft is re-checked with ledeProblems. // 0.18.0 Q08-CONTEXT-1 (owner 2026-10-13: "totally lacking in context and big-picture systems analysis"): each essay gets a BACKGROUND pack of verified reference text (Wikipedia lead sections for the story's subject and recent news coverage) that the writer may cite and the fact-check verifies against; the directive requires a context section and a systems analysis (parties, incentives, constraints, feedback loop, who gains and who pays, where else it operates); the fact-check treats framed analysis that adds no new name, number or event as not a violation, and a reviewer reason calling an item analysis marks it reasoning; readers cap a piece that only retells its source at 3. // 0.17.6 Q08-PRUNE-VERDICT-1 (run 369): the sentence prune sets the verdict line aside and puts it back unchanged, so a verdict that shared a paragraph with the last sentence is no longer damaged into a "missing verdict" failure. // 0.17.5 Q08-LEDE-SPLIT-1 (run 368): an overlong first paragraph is split at a sentence boundary into a lede of at most 70 words and a following paragraph, instead of failing "lede too long"; nothing is added or removed. // 0.17.4 Q08-TICK-BUDGET-1 (agent_issues 2479): an essay after a failed forecast waits for the next tick when more than 240 s are used (run 366 left no row: forecast 256 s + essay up to 644 s reached the 15-minute limit), repair rounds stop at a tick deadline, and every early exit of generate() writes an engine_runs row (skipped or deferred) instead of vanishing. // 0.17.3 Q08-LEDE-1 (fleet audit 2026-10-13): forecasts open with a news lede too (the case they start from, where it was reported, the forecast and its probability) and are checked by ledeProblems; the forecast register is plain explanatory journalism, not the earlier detached register; the site description and home page say what q08 is in plain words. // 0.17.2 PUBLISH-STALL-DIGEST-1 (fleet audit 2026-10-13): the stall issue is rewritten on every stalled tick with the hours, a 24 h breakdown of the stage each run ended at and the newest errors, and turns critical after 24 h; stall failures are logged instead of swallowed. // 0.17.1 Q08-FULLTEXT-1 (agent_issues 2470): the richest thin candidate is written from only when its full text was fetched (at least 600 characters); an abstract-only candidate is skipped with a reported reason. // 0.17.0 Q08-LEDE-1 (owner 2026-10-13): inverted-pyramid essays. The writer is told the source (kind, title, link) and must open with a news lede (who did what, where it appeared, when, why it matters) before the analysis; a deterministic lede check (names the subject, no abstract opening, at most 90 words) joins the writer gate; the editor keeps the lede and reader cuts never remove it; readers score the opening first; titles are plain headlines naming the subject. // 0.16.0 Q08-GITHUB-README-1 (run 358): a GitHub repository source is read from its raw README (the page fetch returned a 378-byte stub, so true README statements were refused as unsupported), falling back to the page when there is none. // 0.15.2 Q08-PARAPHRASE-2 (run 357): a FACT-labelled item whose reason only objects to the wording ("slightly different wording") is a paraphrase too; 0.14.8 read that reason only on unlabelled items. // 0.15.1 Q08-REVISION-FLOOR-1 (run 356): the fact revision is held to the 1500-character prune floor, not the 2800-character writing floor (it rewrites an already pruned or edited draft), so a phrase-level tell is repaired instead of failing with "too short". // 0.15.0 Q08-READER-CUTS-1 (runs 339-355): the paragraph cuts the readers name ("delete paragraphs 4, 6 and 7", "cut the last two paragraphs") are applied deterministically before the model editor, at most half the body and only when the result passes the editor gate; the model editor is the fallback. // 0.14.11 Q08-FLOOR-PARAS-1 (run 354): a pruned or edited draft held to its lower floor needs 4 substantial paragraphs, not the 6 of a full 2800-character draft. // 0.14.10 Q08-PRUNE-FUZZY-1 (run 353): an unsupported item the reviewer paraphrased instead of quoting removes the one sentence sharing most of its content words (at least 4 and 60%), instead of "prune located nothing". // 0.14.9 Q08-TITLE-CARRY-2 (run 352): the title carry (editor drafts and the fact revision) uses every title rule of the gate (label, formula and Title Case titles), not only the dry-title pattern and the length limit. // 0.14.8 Q08-PRUNE-ROUNDS-2 + Q08-PARAPHRASE-1 (run 350): fact repair runs up to 8 rounds while it has used under 6 minutes (5 before), and a reviewer reason that only objects to the wording ("not in source as a direct quote", "not verbatim") marks a paraphrase, not a FACT; "not entirely in source" is read as a negation (it was read as support since 0.14.2). // 0.14.7 Q08-FACT-REVISION-FORMAT-1 (run 349): the fact revision keeps the draft's passing title and verdict line and gets the deterministic framing repair on a phrase-level-only gate failure, instead of failing on a label title it wrote. // 0.14.6 Q08-FACT-SHAPE-2 (runs 347-348): a reviewer item labelled FACT whose quoted text has no digit, quotation or capitalised name and uses generalising wording (wherever, any, every, if, therefore, would, could, pattern, mechanism, incentive) is reasoning; a plain modal claim stays a FACT. // 0.14.5 also removes the title checks 0.14.4-codeagent (PR 1061) pasted ahead of the gate's own if/else chain, which reported every failing title twice. // 0.14.5 Q08-HANDLE-STRIP-1 (run 346): a social handle the writer copied from the source loses its '@' instead of failing the gate (e-mail addresses and @cf/ model ids untouched). Q08-EDITOR-TITLE-CARRY-1 (run 343): an editor draft whose title is missing, over 100 characters or a label title keeps the writer's passing title instead of failing the gate; the body stays the editor's. // 0.14.3 Q08-EDITOR-ROUND-2 + Q08-FACT-OBJECT-ITEM-1 + Q08-FACT-SHAPE-1 (runs 338-341): an unlabelled fact-check item whose quoted text has no digit, quotation or capitalised name and uses causal or modal wording, with a reason that quotes nothing and names no date, figure or name, is reasoning (deleted when the draft can spare it, never a failure); a close panel miss (mean at least 3 of 5) after the editor gets one more editor pass that applies the readers' named cuts, read by a third panel; a fact-check item returned as an object is rebuilt as "LABEL: claim -- reason" instead of "[object Object]". // 0.14.2 Q08-FACT-INFERENCE-2 (run 338): reviewers skipped the FACT/INFERENCE label and wrote 'inference from source' in the reason; an unlabelled item whose reason names an inference, interpretation, generalisation, assumption or paraphrase (and no name, date, number or quotation) is an INFERENCE; the prompt shows an example answer. // 0.14.1 Q08-EDITOR-VERDICT-CARRY-1 (run 337): an editor draft that dropped the final 'worth your time' line gets the writer's line back instead of failing the gate. // 0.14.0 Q08-STALL-RETRY-1 (agent_issues 2286): while no piece has been published for 2 hours, the :30 cron makes a second generation attempt after its audit (same gates and caps, never overlapping the :00 attempt; ops_config q08_stall_retry = off stops it). // 0.13.2 Q08-FACT-INFERENCE-1 (agent_issues 2286): the fact-check labels each finding FACT or INFERENCE; only FACT findings fail a draft (unlabelled ones count as FACT), INFERENCE findings are deleted when the draft can spare them; runs 333-335 failed only because reviewers listed the essay's own reasoning next to real fact errors. // 0.13.0 FRAMING-REPAIR-1 + FACTCHECK-DEGRADE-1 